<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=fire-lance">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fire lance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">950</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as huoqiang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The fire lance was an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapon: a tube of gunpowder, usually bamboo, strapped to a spear or other polearm, with the earliest evidence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 950. Its first recorded use in war came in 1132, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> garrisons used fire lances at the siege of De'an against the Jin. Pellets and shards were later added to the charge, and by the late 13th century metal barrels used without the lance had become the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hand-cannon">hand cannons</a>, making it the ancestor of modern firearms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fire-lance.jpg" alt="Fire lance" width="303" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jiao Yu (焦玉), Huolongjing · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%A2%A8%E8%8A%B1%E9%8E%97.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
